{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Fix pushed for review: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1117 (commit 014a1362d, branch bug-5546-sync-refspec).\n\n**Root cause proven at runtime**, not assumed. Under a narrow clone refspec (`+refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main`) `git fetch origin tickets --quiet` returns rc=0 writing only FETCH_HEAD; `git rev-parse --verify origin/tickets` returns 128; `for-each-ref refs/remotes` lists only `origin/main`. Spying on `sync._union_merge` while driving the real `sync.reconverge` showed it fire ZERO times under the narrow refspec and once under a positive control that created the ref with an explicit refspec — for BOTH an unrelated-history tracker and a genuinely-divergent (shared-ancestry) one. The only variable between the two runs was the existence of `refs/remotes/origin/tickets`, which rules out lock contention, the rebase guard, config resolution and the read-path throttle.\n\n**AC2 (measured end-to-end through `rebar list`, not just a unit test):** a fresh single-branch clone of a project whose upstream store holds 2 tickets, throttle marker cleared between passes —\n- before: pass 1/2/3 -> 0 tickets (exactly the symptom this ticket reports)\n- after: pass 1/2/3 -> 2 tickets\n\n**AC3 (the refusal):** an orphan store carrying its own ticket events is refused with a `DIVERGED` warning naming `rebar fsck` / `rebar fsck-recover`, HEAD untouched (still a single-parent commit), local events intact.\n\nTests: `tests/unit/test_sync_narrow_refspec.py` (new, 3 tests) plus the two retargeted WU-2 tests. All were proven RED before the fix existed and were held out of the implementing subagent's working tree entirely. Both halves of the fix were mutation-checked independently: reverting the explicit refspec returns the two reachability tests to RED; disabling the safety predicate returns the two refusal tests to RED — disjoint subsets, so neither half is a tautology.\n\nSuite: 7669 passed / 32 skipped across tests/unit + tests/interfaces; ruff + mypy clean.\n\nSibling sweep filed as 35f7-a39d-8e27-4922 (linked `discovered_from`): `push.py:182` and the reconcile-bridge workflows carry the same bare-fetch pattern. Notably that is a strong candidate root cause for **449f-f9bf-be90-47fe** and for the Reconcile Bridge push-rejection evidence in the comment above — because `reconcile-bridge.yml:115` creates `refs/remotes/origin/tickets` explicitly but the later fetches at :286/:309 are bare, the ref goes STALE rather than absent, so `rev-list origin/tickets..HEAD` and the reconcile merge operate on a snapshot that never advances and the retry loop provably cannot converge regardless of budget. That mechanism is a cited hypothesis, NOT yet experimentally confirmed against a real runner; confirming it is the first AC on 35f7.\n\nNot closing until CI reports Verified +1."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785517639404798001,"uuid":"75f12ba7-eb06-4617-9ec3-d60e60a07b6b"}